    "failure_scenario": "Every other card zone transition (battlefield→graveyard, →exile, →command, →stack) emits a `card.zoneMoved` observability span with `zone.to` set. A card swallowed into the library never gets meta.zone updated before being deleted, so no `card.zoneMoved` (or any zone-transition) telemetry fires for library returns — Honeycomb traces silently under-report the single most novel interaction this ticket adds, breaking the parity every prior zone transition maintained."
  },
  {
    "file": "apps/tabletop/src/server/sendCardReturned.ts",
    "line": 60,
    "summary": "The JSDoc previously documented (and the old code relied on) an unbounded fetch; it's now replaced with `signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000)`. This changes semantics from 'may hang arbitrarily long but eventually reports the true outcome' to 'gives up at 5s and reports failure even if the Spine would have succeeded.'",
    "failure_scenario": "Under Spine load (GC pause, cold start, DB contention) a request that would have completed in, say, 7 seconds now aborts client-side at 5s. sendCardReturnedToSpineBestEffort returns false, cardReturned.ts responds 502, and the Tabletop client (cardSwallow.ts's completeSwallow) reverts the card to the table. But the already-sent POST body may still be processed server-side by the Spine after the abort, recording a card.returned.v1 event — leaving the Spine's event log and the visible board state permanently out of sync (Spine thinks the card left; the board still shows it in play), with no reconciliation path."
